1. Wheel Positions (module locations)
Code
new Point2D(-6.732283, 4.76378)
These numbers are the X/Y offsets of the module from the robot center, in inches.

To change your wheelbase:

Measure the distance from robot center to each module

Replace these numbers for all four configs

2. Angle Offset
This is the second argument:

Code
0
If your module’s “zero angle” is not straight forward, you adjust this.

3. Servo Direction
Third argument:

Code
Servo.Direction.REVERSE
Leave this unless your servo spins the wrong way.

4. Angle Sensor Direction
Fourth argument:

Code
ServoCoaxialWheelConfig.AngleSenSorDirection.FORWARD
Leave unless your analog angle sensor reads backwards.

5. Steering Gear Ratio (THIS is what you asked about)
This is the fifth argument:

Code
13.7
This is your servo → module steering gear ratio.

If you want a 4:1 ratio, you put:

Code
4.0
If you want 6:1:

Code
6.0
This is the exact place to change it.

 6. Drive Gear Ratio
This is the sixth argument:

Code
105.0 / 32.0
This is your drive motor → wheel gear ratio.

Change this if your drive gearing is different.

 7. Wheel Diameter
This is the seventh argument:

Code
20.0 / 54.0
This is your wheel diameter in inches (converted through their internal scaling).

If you want to set a normal FTC wheel (e.g., 96mm = 3.78in), you would replace this with:

Code
3.78
 8. Max Wheel Speed
This is the last argument:

Code
2.5
This is the maximum wheel speed (ft/s) used for Road Runner constraints.

 Summary: Where to change everything
Physical Value	Where to change it
Wheel positions	new Point2D(x, y)
Steering gear ratio	5th argument in ServoCoaxialWheelConfig
Drive gear ratio	6th argument
Wheel diameter	7th argument
Angle offsets	2nd argument
Servo direction	3rd argument
Angle sensor direction	4th argument

Everything is inside these four blocks:

leftFront

rightFront

leftBack

rightBack